# String-Lattice Décalé

**Ingredients:** LITHUANIAN KANKLĖS + COUPÉ-DÉCALÉ + PREPARED ELECTRIC PIANO + DUNDUN / WOODBLOCK + BASS FLUGELHORN + SHORT ELECTRIC BASS

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a spring residency in a shuttered textile-pattern archive outside Kaunas, where kanklės player Austėja Varnaitė was cataloguing old sutartinės-derived plucking exercises beside shelves of Jacquard punch cards. Abidjan producer Serge Kouamé was working in the same building during a cultural-exchange program, programming stripped coupé-décalé grooves from clipped kicks, handclaps, short toms, whistles, and terse bass. The collision became useful when Serge noticed that Austėja’s most compelling phrases did not behave like melodies. They behaved like woven cells: one string group answered another, the hand crossed the instrument in alternating directions, and open strings continued ringing behind newly plucked notes. Instead of placing the kanklės over a finished club beat, he rebuilt the percussion so each plucked cell occupied one part of a larger interlocking lattice. Prepared electric piano, dundun, woodblock, bass flugelhorn, and short electric bass completed a style where the groove is made from several incomplete patterns that only make sense when heard together.

String-Lattice Décalé is a bright, dry acoustic-electronic dance form built from interlocking Baltic zither cells and the forward-leaning pulse of coupé-décalé. Coupé-décalé supplies the social engine: 118–128 BPM, compact kick, clipped clap, quick low-tom answers, syncopated percussion, short bass punctuation, abrupt chant cues, and a sense of momentum created by repeated rhythmic hooks rather than long harmonic development. The Lithuanian kanklės supplies the tonal architecture through paired plucks, open-string drones, alternating hand direction, selective muting, ringing upper strings, and narrow modal patterns.

The kanklės is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser above the plucking zone, one ribbon microphone toward the lower soundboard, and one restrained stereo room pair. The close condenser captures fingernail edge, string separation, damping noise, and the physical crossing from one string group to another. The ribbon provides body and low-mid warmth. The room pair is kept low and raised only when several open courses are intentionally allowed to overlap.

Players favor Dorian and Mixolydian centers, tonic-fifth drones, suspended seconds, modal thirds, sixths, minor sevenths, pentatonic cells, and repeated three- to seven-note figures. Long folk-style melodies are avoided. The instrument is treated as a grid of available string positions rather than a single lead voice.

The central compositional device is the **Lattice Cell**. A phrase is divided between two or three string zones. Zone A may state notes 1-3-5 while Zone B answers with 2-4-6; neither part forms the full pattern by itself. The listener hears the complete groove only when the fragments interlock across the bar. Some cells are five attacks long, others seven or nine, but they are placed over a stable 4/4 coupé-décalé pulse so their points of coincidence constantly shift.

A second device, **Thread Swap**, keeps the rhythmic pattern but changes which string zone carries each attack. A low tonic that originally appears in Zone A may move to Zone C while the upper suspended-second reply moves down to Zone B. The rhythm remains recognizable while register and resonance change without a conventional chord progression.

A third device, **Open Course**, allows one selected kanklės string to ring across several Lattice Cells while neighboring strings are damped immediately. That sustained course becomes a harmonic thread running through the more percussive plucks. The supporting bass and keyboard are deliberately sparse whenever the Open Course is audible.

A fourth device, **False Downbeat**, shifts the brightest percussion accent away from beat one and places it on the first major coincidence between overlapping kanklės cells. The master pulse never changes, but the ear can temporarily perceive the interlocking acoustic pattern as the true beginning of the bar.

Coupé-décalé drums stay dry and mobile. Kick is compact, generally more frequent than in tarraxo but less continuous than four-on-the-floor house. Clap is short and sharp. Low toms answer the kick in small two- or three-hit figures. High percussion uses shaker, rim, whistle-like transient, or small metal tick, but it is thinned during dense kanklės passages so the string attacks remain legible.

Short electric bass uses root, fifth, octave, sixth, and flat-seventh notes with quick release. It does not trace the kanklės note-for-note. Instead, it lands on structural coincidences: the start of a new Lattice Cell, the end of a Thread Swap, or a False Downbeat. The bass should feel like punctuation, not a continuous foundation.

Prepared electric piano supplies clipped two-note shells and dry rhythmic counterweight. Selected tines are damped with removable felt or paper. Voicings favor suspended seconds, sixths, minor sevenths, open fifths, and octave fragments. The piano is strongest when the kanklės is operating in lower-register cells and nearly disappears when several upper open strings are ringing.

Dundun provides low acoustic authority through short stick-and-bell combinations, restrained bass strokes, muted side hits, and compact rolls. Woodblock marks selected Thread Swaps and False Downbeats. These parts replace individual programmed hits instead of doubling the entire rhythm section.

Bass flugelhorn provides warm low-mid counterlines through soft pedals, short falling thirds, breath-heavy fifths, muted attacks, and small rhythmic answers. It is used mainly when the electric bass rests, allowing the low register to remain active without becoming synthetic or sub-heavy.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Lithuanian, French, Nouchi, or English fragments may reference pattern cards, woven lines, horses cut from paper, dusk shifts, counting marks, or instructions such as “change the thread,” “leave one ringing,” “meet on five,” and “not beat one.” Delivery should be rhythmic, clipped, and conversational, with occasional coupé-décalé-style response shouts rather than a large sung chorus.

Effects remain disciplined. Kanklės stays mostly dry with a short wooden-room reflection and a compact plate only on exposed Open Course tails. Electric piano receives a tiny spring or plate. Bass flugelhorn gets a dark chamber. Percussion remains close and transient-forward. No cinematic Baltic strings, Afro-house pad wash, EDM risers, long 808 glides, synthetic zither doubling, or ornamental “world fusion” layers are allowed.

The defining tension is pattern completeness versus individual incompleteness. No single instrument presents the whole groove. The dance pulse emerges from the points where several partial lines briefly coincide.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 118–128 BPM, primarily 4/4 coupé-décalé architecture with compact kick, clipped clap, short tom answers, syncopated percussion, terse bass punctuation, and kanklės cells of unequal length interlocking across the stable bar.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian, Mixolydian, pentatonic fragments, tonic-fifth drones, suspended seconds, modal thirds, sixths, minor sevenths, and static two-chord fields that leave room for ringing string courses.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Lattice Cell** — incomplete kanklės figures in separate string zones interlock to create the full rhythmic phrase.
2. **Thread Swap** — the rhythm stays fixed while individual attacks migrate to different string registers.
3. **Open Course** — one selected kanklės string is allowed to ring across several dry, damped cells.
4. **False Downbeat** — the brightest percussion accent lands on a major cell coincidence rather than automatically on beat one.
5. **Coincidence Bass** — electric bass and bass flugelhorn enter mainly at points where separate acoustic patterns align.
6. **No-Zither-Loop Rule** — principal kanklės material remains live, segmented, and hand-shaped; no long sampled zither loop may become the harmonic bed.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through denser overlapping cells, shorter gaps between Thread Swaps, stronger clap and tom punctuation, wider register separation, firmer dundun strokes, and more frequent coincidence points. Climaxes stay bright, dry, and dance-forward rather than massive.

# Scene & Origin Lore

String-Lattice Décalé formed inside a shuttered textile-pattern archive and weaving laboratory outside Kaunas. The rooms retained long pattern tables, Jacquard punch cards, numbered thread drawers, wooden bobbins, brass rulers, faded geometric fabric samples, hand-cranked card punches, paper horse cutouts once used for children’s pattern exercises, and tall west-facing windows that filled the archive with orange light late in the day.

Kanklės player **Austėja Varnaitė** had been using the archive to transcribe old multipart rhythmic ideas into playable zither studies. She was interested in patterns that felt incomplete in isolation but became coherent when a second or third line entered.

Abidjan producer **Serge Kouamé** arrived with a compact drum sampler, short electric bass, and a set of pared-back coupé-décalé sketches. His first attempt added kanklės flourishes over a finished rhythm. Austėja disliked the result because the zither had become decoration. She then demonstrated two short string-zone cells that crossed every few beats. Serge muted the percussion at those coincidence points and rebuilt the clap pattern around them. The groove became both more spacious and more danceable.

Prepared-electric-piano player **Rasa Žukaitė** joined after devising clipped harmonic shells that could fill whichever register the current Thread Swap had vacated. Percussionist **Yao N’Guessan** added dundun and woodblock, but only as selective replacements for programmed low toms and sharp accents. Bass-flugelhorn player **Mireille Koffi** completed the ensemble with low brass answers for bass-free passages.

Their first performances were called **pattern evenings**. Audience members could see three narrow horizontal cards above the stage, each punched with a different repeating sequence. The cards did not control electronics. They simply represented the three string zones of the current Lattice Cell.

The visual language developed around linen cream, punch-card brown, oxidized brass, faded ultramarine, persimmon orange, dark kanklės wood, graphite black, and the amber light of late dusk. Paper horses became the group’s recurring emblem after Austėja found a drawer of half-finished children’s cutouts among the archive materials.

The unofficial rule is that if the groove can be understood by soloing one instrument, the arrangement is too simple.

