## Track 2: Paper Horse Crosses the Blue Pattern

**Story & Context:** At 128 BPM, the title-image track uses three simultaneous cells: a seven-attack low kanklės figure, a four-attack upper reply, and a two-note prepared-electric-piano shell. The combination creates shifting coincidence points over an otherwise stable coupé-décalé kick pattern. Serge keeps the bass extremely short, striking only where the seven- and four-attack cells align. Austėja activates Open Course on one upper string, letting it ring while every neighboring note is hand-damped. Yao uses woodblock to mark each Thread Swap and dundun to answer only the strongest three-way alignment. The final section removes clap entirely, leaving the kanklės interlock to imply the forward pulse while crowd-style response shouts appear only once every eight bars.

