## Track 3: Dusk Loom Keeps No Single Count

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 118 BPM and begins with no bass, only kanklės, kick, and a soft dry clap. Austėja alternates a nine-attack cell against a five-attack upper pattern so major coincidences arrive infrequently. Rasa uses isolated minor-seventh electric-piano shells to clarify the tonal center without resolving the rhythmic ambiguity. Mireille plays long breath-heavy flugelhorn tones only during Open Course passages. As the track progresses, Serge removes more percussion rather than adding it, forcing the listener to derive pulse from the interlocking acoustic lines. In the final minute, the two kanklės cells converge briefly into the same five-attack pattern, producing the only obvious unison on the EP. The band then separates again before the final bar. The last sound is one open kanklės course ringing alone into the room.

